#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The privileged half of UI Apply: switch the AES67 service between accepted
profiles on request, verify the result, roll back on failure, leave a receipt.

The web service (usb16x16-web) runs unprivileged with NoNewPrivileges and can
write exactly one thing outside its preset store: a request file in the spool
directory. A root systemd path unit (usb16x16-apply.path) runs this program
whenever that file changes. Nothing in the request is executed; it names an
accepted profile and the profile the browser believed was live, and this
program decides everything else from the live configuration.

One operation, in order, each step recorded in current.json as it happens:

  validating   the request is fresh, unseen, well formed; the profile exists
               in /etc/usb16x16/aes67.conf with STATUS=accepted; the browser's
               expected profile is the live one (a stale page is refused)
  stopping     systemctl stop usb16x16-aes67.service
  switching    usb16x16-aes67-supervisor switch-profile NAME (the supervisor
               validates the block and the preset hash; the service is down,
               so it does not restart anything itself)
  starting     systemctl start usb16x16-aes67.service
  verifying    the supervisor reaches STAGE running, the bridge's telemetry
               line is fresh with zero direction restarts, no RECOVER, and
               the bridge log carries the DSP loaded / engaged lines the
               profile calls for (or none, for a profile without a preset);
               then a settle window with the same checks
  done         or: rolling-back to the previous profile through the same
               steps, then rolled-back, or failed if that also fails

A profile switch restarts audio and draws a new latency level; that is the
operation the user asked for, stated in the UI before the request is sent.
The applier never edits a profile block, never installs a preset, and never
touches the USB gadget, the daemon or the module: switch-profile is the only
write, and it is the supervisor's own command.

A second request kind, "preset" (2026-09-11), installs a user preset the
browser compiled with work/dsp-compiler and switches to it. The artifact text
arrives beside the request in the spool's presets/ directory; this program
recomputes its hash, parses it with the same bounds as the bridge's loader,
requires every biquad section to be stable and the total to stay within the
count the loaded soak passed, loads it through the bridge's own code
(usb16x16-dsp-offline --bench 1), installs it root-owned under
/etc/usb16x16/dsp/user-<slug>.dsp, writes a profile block whose transport
fields are dsp-direct's copied verbatim plus the preset path and hash, has the
supervisor's own parser validate the resulting configuration before it
replaces the live one, and only then runs the switch above. Such a block is
STATUS=experimental (the supervisor's own vocabulary; it switches only to
accepted or experimental profiles) and is identified as a user preset by its
user-<slug> name; it is never accepted: the guards stand in for a soak, and
the UI says so. A third kind, "remove", deletes a user block and its file when it is not
the live profile. Eight user presets at most, one per slug.

Two more kinds (2026-09-13, work/phase4-bridge/LIVE_COEFFICIENTS_DESIGN.md
step 4) update a running user preset without a restart. "live" takes an
artifact the same way "preset" does, runs the same guards, and additionally
requires the live profile to be a user preset whose block carries DSP_LIVE=1
and whose installed file has the same *shape* (every count and index in the
artifact, with gains, delays and coefficients blanked: artifact_shape()
below, the same text bridge-artifact.mjs and DspStage::shape() produce, hashed
with SHA-256). It then writes the artifact to the bridge's live file under
/run/usb16x16 by rename and waits for the bridge's status line to report
that hash applied. Nothing is installed: a restart comes back on the
installed file, by the bridge's own rule (a live file older than the process
is ignored). "commit" copies the applied live file over the installed
user-<slug>.dsp and rewrites the block's DSP_SHA256, through the same
validate-on-a-copy and atomic replace as "preset", with no restart: the
running coefficients already equal the file. Neither kind ever touches an
accepted profile.

A sixth kind, "shutdown" (2026-09-14), powers the Pi off from the page. It
takes the envelope checks only (fresh, unseen, well formed), stops
usb16x16-aes67.service so the bridge and daemon tear down in order before
the rest of the system does, writes its receipt, and then asks systemd for
`systemctl poweroff --no-block`. The receipt is written first on purpose:
it is the last thing the browser can read before the connection drops. The
USB gadget service is not touched; systemd stops it with everything else.
The operator then unplugs the USB data cable, then PoE, as the handoff's
clean shutdown says.

Exit status is 0 when a receipt was written (even for a refused or failed
request: the outcome is the receipt), 2 for a broken environment.
"""

